<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milan Stojadinović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> Stojadinović was a Serbian economist and politician who was prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> from 1935 to 1939. He stabilised the economy, expanded trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and moved the country closer to Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while founding the Yugoslav Radical Union. Dismissed by Prince Paul in 1939, he was interned and handed to the British in 1941, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan-stojadinovic.jpg" alt="Milan Stojadinović" width="200" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_Stojadinovi%C4%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
